For every fixed $\varepsilon>0$, the exponential sum $M_R(\xi)=\sum_{x\in\mathcal C_R}e^{2\pi i x\cdot\xi}$ satisfies $$\liminf_{R\to\infty}\frac1{N_R}
 \int_{|\xi|\le\varepsilon}|M_R(\xi)|^2\,d\xi\ge1.
 \label{energy:fourier-mass}$$

Suppose that on each side the finite pole coefficients, indexed by $L$, have norm at most $a_L$, and the tail coefficients have norm at most $a_T$. The contribution of these coefficients to either $J_i$ has half its absolute second derivative bounded by $a_L\mathcal L(s_0)+a_T\mathcal T(s_0)$ for every $s\ge s_0$, where $$\label{eq:curvature-envelope-table}
\begin{array}{c|rrrrr}
s_0&0&1&2&14.5&23\\\hline
\mathcal L(s_0)&1510&46&14&.00465&.000023\\
\mathcal T(s_0)&26500&1160&800&800&800
\end{array}$$ The transformed constant column has half its absolute second derivative at most $.002$ per unit coefficient on $s\ge14.5$.

For $G(s)=Q_1ze^{(1-s)/z}$, $z>0$, and every $m,v$, $$\label{eq:gaussian-divided-difference}
 U_m[G](v)\ge Q_1e^{(1-m)/z}
 \begin{cases}
 (2z)^{-1},&v\le0,\\[2pt]
 \displaystyle\frac{3z+v}{6z^2+4zv+v^2},&v\ge0.
 \end{cases}$$ Both bounds have their continuous meaning at $v=0$.
